This research extends Fefferman's method of L^2 bounds to a class of tri-linear oscillatory integrals, drawing on Hwang and Jin's approach from [25]. The oscillatory integrals in question are defined as:
  \begin{equation}
  \displaystyle I_{3} f_{1}, f_{2}, f_{3} := \int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}} \int_{\mathbb{R}^{2}} f_{1}(x-y_{1})f_{2}(y_{1}-y_{2})f_{3}(y_{2})e^{2\pi i \phi(t,\xi,\zeta)}\,dy\,dx,
  \end{equation}
  where $\phi(t,\xi,\zeta)$ is a phase function and $f_{1}, f_{2}, f_{3}$ are functions in L^2. The oscillatory integrals of this type appear in the proof of the L^2 decay of the Bergman kernel for domains with finite type, as shown by Bony et al. in [7]. The proof of L^2 bounds for these specific oscillatory integrals involves a decomposition of the oscillatory integrals into simpler forms, with the help of the stationary phase method and L^2 bounds for the resulting expressions.
